
  So the approaches below are mine. The open architectural question is the one your "both, shared module" answer
  created: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a classic global-scope script tag, [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS. Those two cannot consume the same file today,
  and there's no bundler, no transpiler, and no dependencies in this repo. How we bridge that is the decision that
  shapes the rest.

  [1mApproach A — UMD-style shim in the logger core.[0m [38;5;153msrc/logger.js[39m ends with a ~4-line footer: assign to [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m if
  it exists, otherwise hang the factory off [38;5;153mwindow[39m. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains a second script tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m
  keeps its [38;5;153mrequire[39m. Nothing about the existing module syntax changes.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m genuinely one shared file, zero dependencies, zero build step, works over [38;5;153mfile://[39m. The shim is a dated
  idiom and the browser side gets a global. Complexity: trivial.

  [1mApproach B — convert the project to ESM.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gains
  [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m, and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m convert from [38;5;153mrequire[39m/[38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m to [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. Then one
  [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m is imported natively by both.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the modern, clean answer, and it sets the project up properly for later. But it touches three existing
  files that have nothing to do with logging, and module scripts are blocked by CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m — if this page is ever
  opened directly rather than served, the app breaks entirely and the failure looks nothing like a module-system
  problem. Complexity: moderate, and the risk is concentrated in an unknown (how the page is served) rather than in the
  code.

  [1mApproach C — parallel implementations, shared contract.[0m Two small files with an identical interface and no shared
  code; the allowlist and record shape are specified in the spec rather than in a module.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m no shim, no conversion, no changes to existing files. But it duplicates the redaction logic — the one
  piece where drift between two copies means a password reaching a log line. Complexity: trivial, and it's the approach
  that most directly contradicts what you asked for. Listing it for completeness; I don't recommend it.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m It delivers the shared module you asked for at the lowest risk, and it's reversible — if you later
  add a bundler or move to ESM, deleting a four-line footer is the entire migration. B is the better end state but pays
  for it now with unrelated file churn and a [38;5;153mfile://[39m failure mode I can't rule out from here. If you know the page is
  always served over HTTP, B becomes reasonable and I'd be happy to take it.

  Which do you want before I present the detailed design?

